implementationPlan.description:
VS Code half of DoD 1 and DoD 3. Write the tests first, then fix.

(a) Create moe-vscode/tests/powershell-quoting.test.cjs using node:test. Tests share no helper module, so copy what you need:
- from tests/agent-launcher-controls.test.cjs: the launcher harness (transpile; run in a vm with process.platform overridden to win32 and this realm's Error; Emitter, StubWebSocket, loadClient, tempDir, write, extension);
- from tests/accounts-actions.test.cjs: the provider loader (load() with its closed require map, a minimal vscode stub, and AccountPresentation loaded with a throwing require).
Add a JS psWords with the same rule as step 1.

(b) V1 (AgentLauncher.ts; DoD 1 and DoD 3). Launch an architect on win32 with:
- a project that is a real temp directory named Bob’s work, with no .moe, so the launch is legacy;
- an extension directory named ext '‘’‚‛ x, so the script path and both env paths carry all five characters;
- teamName Bob’s ‘team’ ‚x‛ 'y'.
Capture the single sendText line, then assert:
- psWords(whole line) equals [$env:MOE_DAEMON_PATH=, daemon path, ;, $env:MOE_PROXY_PATH=, proxy path, ;, powershell, -NoProfile, -ExecutionPolicy, Bypass, -EncodedCommand, base64];
- decode the last word (base64, utf16le). Its psWords equals [&, script path, -Role, architect, -Project, project dir, -WorkerId, the onLaunched id, -Command, claude, -Team, team];
- the decoded script contains -Project '...Bob’’s work'.

(c) V2 (AccountsTreeProvider.ts; DoD 1). Call accountsCommandLine(cli, file, args, true) with all five characters in node, script, the registry file and one arg. Decode the result. Its psWords equals [&, node, script, --accounts-file, file, ...args].

Each test fails on HEAD with only its own file reverted.

(d) Fix both psQuote copies: `'${String(value).replace(/['‘’‚‛]/g, '$&$&')}'`, plus a one-line comment naming the five characters and EscapeSingleQuotedStringContent. AccountsTreeProvider keeps its own copy because its require map is closed.

Unchanged:
- error handling: assertSafeArg and assertSafeLaunchArgs still run before any quoting;
- the bash shQuote paths;
- the -EncodedCommand framing;
- every existing test file.

Gate, DoD 4. Run everything in snapshots outside the checkout (global rail). Hold one jetbrains-gradle lease for all Gradle runs (mem:convention-jetbrains-gradle-lease), and release it right after, even on failure. Never kill java or gradle processes by name.

(1) RED. Take a git-archive HEAD snapshot and overlay ONLY the two test files.
- Kotlin: .\gradlew.bat test --tests com.moe.util.TerminalAgentLauncherTest --rerun must fail J1, J2 and J3, and pass every pre-existing test.
- VS Code: after npm ci in the snapshot, node --test tests/powershell-quoting.test.cjs must fail V1 and V2.

(2) Mutants. Write them only into snapshot copies (mem:pattern-mutant-sweep-in-gate-snapshot). Each must fail a named test:
- Kotlin psQuote doubling only the ASCII quote: J1 and J3;
- the quote set without U+201A: J1;
- powerShellLine without the backtick: J2;
- powerShellLine without U+201E: J2;
- chained replaces that escape " before the backtick: J2;
- HEAD's bashWrapPowerShell un-escape: J2, WSL rows;
- each VS Code psQuote back to ASCII-only, one at a time: V1 alone, then V2 alone;
- the VS Code quote set without U+201B: V1 and V2.

(3) Run the checkRef verbatim; it must exit 0:
R=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel) && cd "$R" && F="moe-vscode/src/util/AgentLauncher.ts moe-vscode/src/providers/AccountsTreeProvider.ts moe-vscode/tests/powershell-quoting.test.cjs moe-jetbrains/src/main/kotlin/com/moe/util/TerminalAgentLauncher.kt moe-jetbrains/src/test/kotlin/com/moe/util/TerminalAgentLauncherTest.kt" && V=$(mktemp -d -t psq2f4b-vsc-XXXXXX) && git archive HEAD | tar -x -C "$V" && cp --parents $F "$V/" && (cd "$V/moe-vscode" && umask 022 && env -i HOME="$HOME" PATH="$HOME/.nvm/versions/node/v24.21.0/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin" bash -c 'npm ci --no-audit --no-fund && npm run lint && npm run compile && npm test') && J=/mnt/d/moe-scratch/psq2f4b-jb-gate && rm -rf "$J" && mkdir -p "$J" && git archive HEAD | tar -x -C "$J" && cp --parents $F "$J/" && cd /mnt/d/moe-scratch && cmd.exe /d /c 'set GRADLE_OPTS=-Xmx512m&& cd /d D:\moe-scratch\psq2f4b-jb-gate\moe-jetbrains && .\gradlew.bat test --rerun'
- Confirm "> Task :test" ran and was not UP-TO-DATE.
- Read the TerminalAgentLauncherTest count and the JUnit total from the XML (mem:gotcha-gradle-uptodate-inside-chained-gate).

Record in complete_task: the RED failures, the mutant kills, the VS Code test pass/fail/skip counts and the JUnit counts.

Adversarial self-review: one pass, at the end, scoped to this row's diff (this is a mid-epic row). Check:
- the three psQuote copies hold the same five characters (diff them);
- powerShellLine is a single pass, and bashWrapPowerShell is its exact inverse;
- git diff --stat names only the 5 planned files;
- no existing test changed; PINNED_LEGACY, the accounts pins and AccountPresentationTest are byte-identical;
- requireSafeArg and assertSafeArg still run first;
- no new export;
- size stays under the 400-line cap.

In complete_task, state these residuals as pre-existing and out of scope. Both are filed as task-76d4500abc1047d5b51d63f2a2dc92bc:
- (a) Windows PowerShell 5.1's native argument passing drops a plain ASCII " from JetBrains' -Command string. A probe at planning showed the value stays in its own argument, so this is not a break-out.
- (b) In an interactive bash, ! inside the WSL wrap's double quotes triggers history expansion.

planningNotes.approachesConsidered:
Outer layer, (A) chosen: one-pass backtick escaping in powerShellLine, with an exact-inverse un-escape in bashWrapPowerShell. It fixes every DoD class and keeps every existing pin byte-identical.

(B) -EncodedCommand, as VS Code does, was rejected for this row. It also fixes two pre-existing gaps (a plain ASCII " dropped on Windows PowerShell 5.1, and bash ! history expansion), but it costs more:
- it re-pins PINNED_LEGACY's 8 PowerShell rows, the accounts pin and AccountPresentationTest (a sixth file);
- about 15 contains-assertions would pass vacuously on an encoded line unless each decodes first;
- it lands near the 400-line cap;
- JetBrains launch lines become opaque.
B is filed as BACKLOG task-76d4500abc1047d5b51d63f2a2dc92bc.

psQuote doubles each single-quote character as itself, as PowerShell does. Mapping curly quotes to ASCII would change the value.

planningNotes.codebaseInsights:
Planning probe (2026-11-02, Windows PowerShell 5.1.26100 from WSL, harmless Write-Output payloads; each value was echoed back as code points). With (A) and the five-character psQuote, every value arrived intact on the native route and on the WSL interop route:
- D:\Bob’s work;
- the PWNED1, PWNED2 and PWNED3 probes;
- a`nb`$c;
- $(...)$env:USERNAME;
- all seven curly quotes;
- a trailing backtick.
The one exception: the native route dropped the plain ASCII " of x`"... The value still stayed in one argument.

At HEAD:
- PWNED1, PWNED2 and PWNED3 executed;
- D:\Bob’s work was a parse error;
- `n became a newline;
- a trailing backtick was lost.

Where the code differs between plugins:
- In VS Code, the $env:K='v'; prefix is typed in the OUTER shell. JetBrains puts it inside the -Command script.
- AgentLauncher's psQuote is private, so tests reach it through launchAgent on win32 with the vm platform override, as agent-launcher-controls.test.cjs does.
- AccountsTreeProvider exports accountsCommandLine, and loading the module touches no vscode API.
- In JetBrains, the WSL-wrapped PowerShell route keeps the Windows basePath. Only the BASH kind converts to /mnt.
